## Summary of what the survey actually shows

**Nobody in this set uses `deny_unknown_fields` on a wire type. Zero occurrences across all five codebases.** Casing splits cleanly by lineage: formats derived from a non-Rust spec (OCI, LSP, SARIF, protobuf JSON) are `camelCase`; formats native to Rust tooling (cargo metadata, cargo `--message-format=json`, rustc diagnostics) are `snake_case`. Forward compat is bought with **open enums + `Option`/`#[serde(default)]`**, never with strict parsing.

---

## 1. oci-spec-rs (`containers/oci-spec-rs`, v0.10.0)

Clone: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/containers/oci-spec-rs`

**`deny_unknown_fields`: 0 occurrences in the entire repo** (`grep -rn deny_unknown_fields` → empty).
**`#[non_exhaustive]`: exactly 1**, and it's on a non-serde type (`DigestAlgorithm`).

`rename_all` census (`src/`):
| casing | count | where |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `camelCase` | ~50 | all image + runtime structs |
| `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` | 7 | `LinuxCapability`, `Signal`, `Arch` (seccomp), `distribution/error.rs` |
| `PascalCase` | 1 | `image::Config` (Docker-inherited) |
| `lowercase` | 4 | `LinuxNamespaceType`, `ContainerState`, zos |

`src/image/descriptor.rs:8-11`:
```rust
#[derive(
    Builder, Clone, CopyGetters, Debug, Deserialize, Eq, Getters, Setters, PartialEq, Serialize,
)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
```
That is the *entire* container attribute set. Same at `src/image/manifest.rs:29`, `src/image/index.rs:22`, `src/image/oci_layout.rs:14`, `src/image/artifact.rs:15`.

The one PascalCase exception, `src/image/config.rs:284` — the Docker-legacy `Config` object whose JSON keys are `User`, `Env`, `Cmd`:
```rust
#[serde(rename_all = "PascalCase")]
```
And spec-literal names override the convention rather than bending it (`src/image/config.rs:69,77`):
```rust
#[serde(rename = "os.version", skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
os_version: Option<String>,
#[serde(rename = "os.features", skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
os_features: Option<Vec<String>>,
```

**Enum representation — this is the load-bearing finding.** `MediaType`, `Os`, and `Arch` are *not* derived serde enums. They are hand-written open enums with an `Other(String)` escape hatch and manual `Serialize`/`Deserialize` that round-trip through `String` (`src/image/mod.rs:28-72, 165-182`):
```rust
/// Media types used by OCI image format spec. Values MUST comply with RFC 6838,
/// including the naming requirements in its section 4.2.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum MediaType {
    Descriptor,
    LayoutHeader,
    ImageManifest,
    // ...
    /// MediaType not specified by OCI image format.
    Other(String),
}

impl Serialize for MediaType {
    fn serialize<S>(&self, serializer: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error>
    where S: serde::Serializer {
        let media_type = format!("{self}");
        media_type.serialize(serializer)
    }
}

impl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for MediaType {
    fn deserialize<D>(deserializer: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error>
    where D: serde::Deserializer<'de> {
        let media_type = String::deserialize(deserializer)?;
        Ok(media_type.as_str().into())   // infallible; unknown -> Other(..)
    }
}
```
`Os` (`src/image/mod.rs:188`) and `Arch` (`src/image/mod.rs:291`) are identical in shape, each ending in `Other(String)`. **Deserializing an unknown media type / OS / arch cannot fail.**

`DigestAlgorithm` is the only `#[non_exhaustive]`, and it states the rationale explicitly (`src/image/digest.rs:5-25`):
```rust
/// Other digest algorithms may be added
/// in the future, so this structure is marked as non-exhaustive.
#[non_exhaustive]
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub enum DigestAlgorithm {
    Sha256, Sha384, Sha512,
    /// Any other algorithm. Note that it is possible
    /// that other algorithms will be added as enum members.
    Other(Box<str>),
}
```
Note it carries **both** `#[non_exhaustive]` *and* `Other(_)` — belt and braces.

**Documented tri-state rationale** (`src/runtime/features.rs:38-46`):
```rust
/// The list of the recognized hook names, e.g., "createRuntime".
/// "None" means "unknown", not "no support for any hook".
hooks: Option<Vec<String>>,
/// The list of the recognized mount options, e.g., "ro".
/// "None" means "unknown", not "no support for any mount option".
mount_options: Option<Vec<String>>,
```

The upstream spec requirement they're encoding, quoted in their own doc comment at `src/image/manifest.rs:54-58`: *"Implementations storing or copying image manifests MUST NOT error on encountering an artifactType that is unknown to the implementation."*

No README/CONTRIBUTING rationale — the policy lives in doc comments and in the shape of the types.

---

## 2. lsp-types (`gluon-lang/lsp-types`)

**`deny_unknown_fields`: 0. `#[non_exhaustive]`: 0.**
`rename_all` census: **289 × `camelCase`**, 10 × `lowercase`, 2 × `camelCase` combined with `untagged`.
Also: **53 `#[serde(untagged)]`**, **149 `#[serde(flatten)]`**, **179 `skip_serializing_if`** in `src/lib.rs` alone.

Typical container attrs (`src/lib.rs:947-948`) — again, nothing but casing:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Clone, Deserialize, Serialize, Default)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
pub struct InitializeParams {
    pub process_id: Option<u32>,
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    #[deprecated(note = "Use `root_uri` instead when possible")]
    pub root_path: Option<String>,
    #[serde(default)]
    #[deprecated(note = "Use `workspace_folders` instead when possible")]
    pub root_uri: Option<Uri>,
```

**Union types → `#[serde(untagged)]`** (`src/lib.rs:203-208`, `733-738`, `755-759`):
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq, Clone, Deserialize, Serialize)]
#[serde(untagged)]
pub enum NumberOrString { Number(i32), String(String) }

#[serde(untagged)]
pub enum DocumentChanges { Edits(Vec<TextDocumentEdit>), Operations(Vec<DocumentChangeOperation>) }
```

**LSP's integer enums are modeled as open newtypes, not Rust enums** — the crate's whole forward-compat story (`src/lib.rs:1119-1152`):
```rust
/// A symbol kind.
#[derive(Eq, PartialEq, Copy, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(transparent)]
pub struct SymbolKind(i32);
lsp_enum! {
impl SymbolKind {
    pub const FILE: SymbolKind = SymbolKind(1);
    pub const MODULE: SymbolKind = SymbolKind(2);
    // ... through TYPE_PARAMETER = 26
}
}
```
An unrecognized `SymbolKind(99)` deserializes fine; the generated `Debug` impl has a `_ => write!(f, "{}({})", stringify!($typ), self.0)` arm (`src/lib.rs:70-79`). Only the *string* `TryFrom<&str>` conversion can error.

The spec rationale is carried verbatim in the doc comment (`src/lib.rs:1154-1166`):
```rust
/// Specific capabilities for the `SymbolKind` in the `workspace/symbol` request.
pub struct SymbolKindCapability {
    /// The symbol kind values the client supports. When this
    /// property exists the client also guarantees that it will
    /// handle values outside its set gracefully and falls back
    /// to a default value when unknown.
    pub value_set: Option<Vec<SymbolKind>>,
}
```

There's also a revealing comment at `src/lib.rs:740-753` — they *wanted* `#[serde(other)]` on an internally-tagged enum for the fallback case and couldn't have it (serde-rs/serde#912), so they fell back to `untagged`:
```rust
// TODO: Once https://github.com/serde-rs/serde/issues/912 is solved
// we can remove ResourceOp and switch to the following implementation
// of DocumentChangeOperation:
//
// #[serde(tag = "kind", rename_all="lowercase" )]
// pub enum DocumentChangeOperation {
//     Create(CreateFile), Rename(RenameFile), Delete(DeleteFile),
//     #[serde(other)]
//     Edit(TextDocumentEdit),
// }
```

README CONTRIBUTING section says only: *"If you are making a change which adds, removes or modifies the LSP API it is highly appreciated if you link to the spec where this change is described."*

---

## 3. cargo_metadata (`oli-obk/cargo_metadata`)

**`deny_unknown_fields`: 0. `#[non_exhaustive]`: 25** — on essentially every public wire struct.
`rename_all` census: **only 5 total** — 1 × `kebab-case`, 4 × `lowercase`. **No `rename_all` on any struct.** Fields serialize as Rust `snake_case`, because cargo's JSON *is* snake_case (`package_id`, `manifest_path`, `workspace_members`, `target_directory`).

`src/lib.rs:257-289`:
```rust
#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
#[cfg_attr(feature = "builder", derive(Builder))]
#[non_exhaustive]
#[cfg_attr(feature = "builder", builder(pattern = "owned", setter(into)))]
/// Starting point for metadata returned by `cargo metadata`
pub struct Metadata {
    pub packages: Vec<Package>,
    pub workspace_members: Vec<PackageId>,
    /// This is not available if running with a version of Cargo older than 1.71.
    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "WorkspaceDefaultMembers::is_missing")]
    pub workspace_default_members: WorkspaceDefaultMembers,
    ...
}
```

**Newly-added upstream fields get `#[serde(default)]` plus a version note** — this is the forward-compat convention, repeated throughout (`src/messages.rs:210-214`, `src/lib.rs` `NodeDep`):
```rust
    /// The `OUT_DIR` environment variable where this script places its output
    ///
    /// Added in Rust 1.41.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub out_dir: Utf8PathBuf,
```
```rust
    /// The kinds of dependencies.
    ///
    /// This field was added in Rust 1.41.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub dep_kinds: Vec<DepKindInfo>,
```

**Three distinct open-enum idioms, all in one crate:**

`#[serde(other)]` catch-all (`src/dependency.rs:13-29`):
```rust
pub enum DependencyKind {
    #[serde(rename = "normal")] #[default] Normal,
    #[serde(rename = "dev")] Development,
    #[serde(rename = "build")] Build,
    #[doc(hidden)]
    #[serde(other)]
    Unknown,
}
```

`#[serde(untagged)]` string fallback + `#[non_exhaustive]` + a written policy in the doc comment (`src/lib.rs:771-817`):
```rust
/// Kind of target.
/// ...
/// Other possible values may be added in the future.
#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, PartialOrd, Ord)]
#[non_exhaustive]
pub enum TargetKind {
    #[serde(rename = "bench")] Bench,
    #[serde(rename = "bin")] Bin,
    #[serde(rename = "custom-build")] CustomBuild,
    ...
    /// Unknown type
    #[serde(untagged)]
    Unknown(String),
}
```

Hand-rolled fallback with *two* unknown arms, and the rationale spelled out (`src/messages.rs:38-56`):
```rust
#[non_exhaustive]
pub enum ArtifactDebuginfo {
    #[default] None, LineDirectivesOnly, LineTablesOnly, Limited, Full,
    /// An unknown integer level.
    ///
    /// This may be produced by a version of rustc in the future that has
    /// additional levels represented by an integer that are not known by this
    /// version of `cargo_metadata`.
    UnknownInt(i64),
    /// An unknown string level.
    ///
    /// This may be produced by a version of rustc in the future that has
    /// additional levels represented by a string that are not known by this
    /// version of `cargo_metadata`.
    UnknownString(String),
}
```

Top-level message dispatch (`src/messages.rs:225-245`):
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
#[non_exhaustive]
#[serde(tag = "reason", rename_all = "kebab-case")]
pub enum Message {
    CompilerArtifact(Artifact),
    CompilerMessage(CompilerMessage),
    BuildScriptExecuted(BuildScript),
    /// Added in Rust 1.44.
    BuildFinished(BuildFinished),
    /// A line of text which isn't a cargo or compiler message.
    #[serde(skip)]
    TextLine(String),
}
```
`#[non_exhaustive]` on `Message` is exactly the "an unknown `reason` may appear" hedge — combined with `TextLine(String)` for non-JSON stdout lines.

README and CHANGELOG contain **no** written policy statement about field addition. The policy is encoded structurally: `#[non_exhaustive]` on every struct means adding a public field is not a semver-major change (see §7 citation below).

---

## 4. scip (`sourcegraph/scip`, rust bindings v0.10.0)

**There is no serde in the Rust bindings at all.** `grep -rn serde bindings/rust/` → empty. `bindings/rust/Cargo.toml`:
```toml
[dependencies]
# Must match the protoc-gen-rust version used for code generation (see flake.nix)
protobuf = "=3.7.2"
```
Generated by `rust-protobuf 3.7.2` (`bindings/rust/src/generated/scip.rs:1-3`). The forward-compat mechanism is protobuf's, not serde's — every message carries an unknown-field bucket:
```rust
pub struct Index {
    pub metadata: ::protobuf::MessageField<Metadata>,
    pub documents: ::std::vec::Vec<Document>,
    pub external_symbols: ::std::vec::Vec<SymbolInformation>,
    pub special_fields: ::protobuf::SpecialFields,
}
```
(`SpecialFields` = `UnknownFields` + `CachedSize`.)

`scip.proto` uses `lower_snake_case` field names and reserves removed numbers explicitly (`scip.proto:246-248`):
```protobuf
  // Reserved field numbers from the Document message to prevent accidental
  // reuse, which would break wire compatibility with older indexes.
  reserved 1, 3, 6;
```
Enums leave room to grow: `// Next = 87;` (line 425), `// NextLanguage = 112;` (line 955). No `json_name` overrides anywhere. `docs/CLI.md` offers `--json` output with the warning *"Do not rely on non-JSON output in scripts"* — i.e. JSON is the stable surface, and its casing is whatever ProtoJSON mandates: **lowerCamelCase** (§7).

---

## 5. Cargo's machine-readable JSON policy — correction to the brief

**The "new fields may be added" quote is in rustc's docs, not cargo's.** I checked `https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/external-tools.html` and grepped its rendered text for `compatib|new field|unknown|stab`; there is no such sentence. What cargo's page actually says, verbatim:

> The format is stable and versioned. When calling `cargo metadata`, you should pass `--format-version` flag explicitly to avoid forward incompatibility hazard.

and:

> The output goes to stdout in the JSON object per line format. The `reason` field distinguishes different kinds of messages.

Cargo's registry API page *does* carry the ignore-unknown rule — https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/registry-web-api.html:

> For backwards compatibility, servers should ignore any unexpected query parameters or JSON fields. If a JSON field is missing, it should be assumed to be null. The endpoints are versioned with the `v1` component of the path, and Cargo is responsible for handling backwards compatibility fallbacks should any be required in the future.

---

## 6. rustc's JSON output — the exact citation you wanted

https://doc.rust-lang.org/rustc/json.html, source at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rust-lang/rust/master/src/doc/rustc/src/json.md` lines 14-18, verbatim:

> Each type of message has a `$message_type` field which can be used to distinguish the different formats. When parsing, care should be taken to be forwards-compatible with future changes to the format. Optional values may be `null`. New fields may be added. Enumerated fields like "level" or "suggestion_applicability" may add new values.

And line 320:

> Note that the JSON format of the `timings` messages is unstable and subject to change.

Casing of the actual payload is **snake_case** (`json.md:60-80`): `file_name`, `byte_start`, `byte_end`, `line_start`, `line_end`, `suggested_replacement`, `suggestion_applicability`, plus the sigil field `$message_type`. Enumerated values are kebab/lower strings: `"failure-note"`, `"MachineApplicable"` (the applicability one is PascalCase — inconsistent, deliberately open).

---

## 7. Written, citable guidance

**serde's own docs** — https://serde.rs/container-attrs.html, `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`:

> Always error during deserialization when encountering unknown fields. When this attribute is not present, by default unknown fields are ignored for self-describing formats like JSON.

That's the key point: **serde's default is already the forward-compatible behavior.** `deny_unknown_fields` is opt-in strictness. Same page enumerates the exact `rename_all` values: `"lowercase"`, `"UPPERCASE"`, `"PascalCase"`, `"camelCase"`, `"snake_case"`, `"SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE"`, `"kebab-case"`, `"SCREAMING-KEBAB-CASE"`. Serde takes **no position** on which to pick for JSON.

**Protobuf JSON mapping** — https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/json/:

> Message field names are mapped to lowerCamelCase to be used as JSON object keys.
> Parsers accept both the lowerCamelCase name (or the one specified by the `json_name` option) and the original proto field name.
> The protobuf JSON parser should reject unknown fields by default but may provide an option to ignore unknown fields in parsing.

Worth flagging the tension: ProtoJSON is the one spec here that *mandates* strictness on unknown fields — but only because the binary wire format preserves them and JSON is treated as a debug/interop surface. https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto3/:

> Adding new fields is safe. If you add new fields, any messages serialized by code using your "old" message format can still be parsed by your new generated code.
> Proto3 messages preserve unknown fields and include them during parsing and in the serialized output, which matches proto2 behavior.

and the explicit caveat that JSON breaks this — unknown fields are lost when you *"Serialize a proto to JSON"*, with the advice *"Use binary; avoid using text formats for data exchange."* So citing ProtoJSON's reject-by-default as precedent for a JSON-native format is a misread of why it's there.

**Google API Design Guide, AIP-140** — https://google.aip.dev/140:

> Field definitions in protobuf files **must** use `lower_snake_case` names.

…which are "mapped to an appropriate naming convention in JSON and in generated code" — i.e. the design-guide position is *declare snake_case, emit camelCase*, mechanically.

**Cargo SemVer reference** — https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/semver.html. This is the citation that explains why `cargo_metadata` puts `#[non_exhaustive]` on 25 types:

> **Major: adding a public field when no private field exists.** When a public field is added to a struct that has all public fields, this will break any code that attempts to construct it with a struct literal.
> **Major: adding new enum variants (without `non_exhaustive`).** It is a breaking change to add a new enum variant if the enum does not use the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute.
> Mitigation: When introducing the enum, mark it as `#[non_exhaustive]` to force users to use wildcard patterns to catch new variants.

Note this is about *Rust API* semver, not wire semver — but it's the reason a wire-type crate that expects to gain fields marks its structs `#[non_exhaustive]`.

**Rust API Guidelines, future-proofing** — https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/future-proofing.html. C-STRUCT-PRIVATE:

> Making a field public is a strong commitment: it pins down a representation choice, _and_ prevents the type from providing any validation or maintaining any invariants on the contents of the field, since clients can mutate it arbitrarily.

The guidelines have **nothing** on JSON casing and nothing directly on `#[non_exhaustive]` (predates it).

**JSON Schema** — https://json-schema.org/understanding-json-schema/reference/object, under "Extending Closed Schemas":

> It's important to note that `additionalProperties` only recognizes properties declared in the same subschema as itself. So, `additionalProperties` can restrict you from "extending" a schema using combining keywords such as `allOf`.

i.e. the schema-level equivalent of `deny_unknown_fields` is documented as an *obstacle to extension*, with `unevaluatedProperties` (draft 2019-09) offered as the escape.

**A concrete production incident report on `deny_unknown_fields`** — https://github.com/aws/aws-lambda-rust-runtime/issues/750 (ramosbugs, 2023-12-12):

> Using this serde attribute causes deserialization to fail if any unexpected fields are included in the request payload.
> If the Amazon API Gateway team were to add a new feature that involved a new request field, all deployed Rust Lambda functions using the Lambda proxy integration might immediately break in production. In most APIs, adding a new field is not considered a breaking change.

**RFC 9413, "Maintaining Robust Protocols"** — https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9413.html. Useful as the *counterweight*: it argues that leniency-by-default is not an extensibility strategy, and that extensibility should be designed explicitly (§2.2):

> Using undocumented or accidental features of a protocol as the basis of an extensibility mechanism can be extremely difficult.

The synthesis both sides support: don't rely on accidental tolerance, but *do* specify "unknown members are ignored" as a rule, and give enums an explicit `Other`/unknown carrier so the tolerance is a documented feature rather than an accident. That is precisely what oci-spec-rs, lsp-types, and cargo_metadata each do.

---

## 8. Bonus data point — serde-sarif (`psastras/sarif-rs`)

A stable, externally-specified JSON format (OASIS SARIF 2.1.0) generated into Rust via `schemafy_lib` from `serde-sarif/src/schema.json`. **`deny_unknown_fields`: 0. `#[non_exhaustive]`: 0. 11 × `rename_all = "camelCase"` on the enums**, e.g. `serde-sarif/src/sarif.rs:21-24`:
```rust
#[doc = "The role or roles played by the artifact in the analysis."]
#[derive(Display, Debug, Serialize, Deserialize, EnumString)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
#[strum(serialize_all = "camelCase")]
pub enum ArtifactRoles { AnalysisTarget, Attachment, ... }
```
The interesting bit: the bundled SARIF schema contains **52 instances of `"additionalProperties": false`** — the format is officially closed — yet the Rust binding does not enforce it. Even a spec that says "closed" gets a deserializer that shrugs at unknown fields.

---

## Practical read-out for a Wisp wire format

- **Casing:** camelCase only if you're mirroring a non-Rust spec. If Wisp's JSON is Rust-native tooling output (like cargo/rustc), plain `snake_case` with no `rename_all` is the majority precedent and removes a whole class of rename drift. Pick one and put it in the contract doc; every crate above is internally consistent and deviates only where a spec forces it (`os.version`, `PascalCase` Docker `Config`).
- **`deny_unknown_fields`: don't.** 0/5 use it; serde's default is already right; there's a real production-outage report against it.
- **`#[non_exhaustive]`:** it's about *Rust* API semver, not wire semver. cargo_metadata puts it on everything so it can add fields in a patch release; oci-spec-rs mostly skips it because its structs have private fields + builders (which achieves the same thing). Pick one of those two mechanisms.
- **Enums are the actual forward-compat risk**, not fields. Every crate here made its wire enums open: `Other(String)` (OCI), `#[serde(transparent)] struct Kind(i32)` + consts (LSP), `#[serde(other)]` / `#[serde(untagged)] Unknown(String)` / `UnknownInt|UnknownString` (cargo_metadata). A bare derived `enum` with no fallback arm is the one thing none of them ship on a wire type.
- **Say it in prose too.** rustc's one sentence — *"Optional values may be `null`. New fields may be added. Enumerated fields ... may add new values."* — is the model for a contract note; it's what makes the leniency a specified feature rather than an accident (RFC 9413's point).

## Summary of what the survey actually shows

**Nobody in this set uses `deny_unknown_fields` on a wire type. Zero occurrences across all five codebases.** Casing splits cleanly by lineage: formats derived from a non-Rust spec (OCI, LSP, SARIF, protobuf JSON) are `camelCase`; formats native to Rust tooling (cargo metadata, cargo `--message-format=json`, rustc diagnostics) are `snake_case`. Forward compat is bought with **open enums + `Option`/`#[serde(default)]`**, never with strict parsing.

---

## 1. oci-spec-rs (`containers/oci-spec-rs`, v0.10.0)

Clone: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/containers/oci-spec-rs`

**`deny_unknown_fields`: 0 occurrences in the entire repo** (`grep -rn deny_unknown_fields` → empty).
**`#[non_exhaustive]`: exactly 1**, and it's on a non-serde type (`DigestAlgorithm`).

`rename_all` census (`src/`):
| casing | count | where |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `camelCase` | ~50 | all image + runtime structs |
| `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` | 7 | `LinuxCapability`, `Signal`, `Arch` (seccomp), `distribution/error.rs` |
| `PascalCase` | 1 | `image::Config` (Docker-inherited) |
| `lowercase` | 4 | `LinuxNamespaceType`, `ContainerState`, zos |

`src/image/descriptor.rs:8-11`:
```rust
#[derive(
    Builder, Clone, CopyGetters, Debug, Deserialize, Eq, Getters, Setters, PartialEq, Serialize,
)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
```
That is the *entire* container attribute set. Same at `src/image/manifest.rs:29`, `src/image/index.rs:22`, `src/image/oci_layout.rs:14`, `src/image/artifact.rs:15`.

The one PascalCase exception, `src/image/config.rs:284` — the Docker-legacy `Config` object whose JSON keys are `User`, `Env`, `Cmd`:
```rust
#[serde(rename_all = "PascalCase")]
```
And spec-literal names override the convention rather than bending it (`src/image/config.rs:69,77`):
```rust
#[serde(rename = "os.version", skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
os_version: Option<String>,
#[serde(rename = "os.features", skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
os_features: Option<Vec<String>>,
```

**Enum representation — this is the load-bearing finding.** `MediaType`, `Os`, and `Arch` are *not* derived serde enums. They are hand-written open enums with an `Other(String)` escape hatch and manual `Serialize`/`Deserialize` that round-trip through `String` (`src/image/mod.rs:28-72, 165-182`):
```rust
/// Media types used by OCI image format spec. Values MUST comply with RFC 6838,
/// including the naming requirements in its section 4.2.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum MediaType {
    Descriptor,
    LayoutHeader,
    ImageManifest,
    // ...
    /// MediaType not specified by OCI image format.
    Other(String),
}

impl Serialize for MediaType {
    fn serialize<S>(&self, serializer: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error>
    where S: serde::Serializer {
        let media_type = format!("{self}");
        media_type.serialize(serializer)
    }
}

impl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for MediaType {
    fn deserialize<D>(deserializer: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error>
    where D: serde::Deserializer<'de> {
        let media_type = String::deserialize(deserializer)?;
        Ok(media_type.as_str().into())   // infallible; unknown -> Other(..)
    }
}
```
`Os` (`src/image/mod.rs:188`) and `Arch` (`src/image/mod.rs:291`) are identical in shape, each ending in `Other(String)`. **Deserializing an unknown media type / OS / arch cannot fail.**

`DigestAlgorithm` is the only `#[non_exhaustive]`, and it states the rationale explicitly (`src/image/digest.rs:5-25`):
```rust
/// Other digest algorithms may be added
/// in the future, so this structure is marked as non-exhaustive.
#[non_exhaustive]
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub enum DigestAlgorithm {
    Sha256, Sha384, Sha512,
    /// Any other algorithm. Note that it is possible
    /// that other algorithms will be added as enum members.
    Other(Box<str>),
}
```
Note it carries **both** `#[non_exhaustive]` *and* `Other(_)` — belt and braces.

**Documented tri-state rationale** (`src/runtime/features.rs:38-46`):
```rust
/// The list of the recognized hook names, e.g., "createRuntime".
/// "None" means "unknown", not "no support for any hook".
hooks: Option<Vec<String>>,
/// The list of the recognized mount options, e.g., "ro".
/// "None" means "unknown", not "no support for any mount option".
mount_options: Option<Vec<String>>,
```

The upstream spec requirement they're encoding, quoted in their own doc comment at `src/image/manifest.rs:54-58`: *"Implementations storing or copying image manifests MUST NOT error on encountering an artifactType that is unknown to the implementation."*

No README/CONTRIBUTING rationale — the policy lives in doc comments and in the shape of the types.

---

## 2. lsp-types (`gluon-lang/lsp-types`)

**`deny_unknown_fields`: 0. `#[non_exhaustive]`: 0.**
`rename_all` census: **289 × `camelCase`**, 10 × `lowercase`, 2 × `camelCase` combined with `untagged`.
Also: **53 `#[serde(untagged)]`**, **149 `#[serde(flatten)]`**, **179 `skip_serializing_if`** in `src/lib.rs` alone.

Typical container attrs (`src/lib.rs:947-948`) — again, nothing but casing:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Clone, Deserialize, Serialize, Default)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
pub struct InitializeParams {
    pub process_id: Option<u32>,
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    #[deprecated(note = "Use `root_uri` instead when possible")]
    pub root_path: Option<String>,
    #[serde(default)]
    #[deprecated(note = "Use `workspace_folders` instead when possible")]
    pub root_uri: Option<Uri>,
```

**Union types → `#[serde(untagged)]`** (`src/lib.rs:203-208`, `733-738`, `755-759`):
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq, Clone, Deserialize, Serialize)]
#[serde(untagged)]
pub enum NumberOrString { Number(i32), String(String) }

#[serde(untagged)]
pub enum DocumentChanges { Edits(Vec<TextDocumentEdit>), Operations(Vec<DocumentChangeOperation>) }
```

**LSP's integer enums are modeled as open newtypes, not Rust enums** — the crate's whole forward-compat story (`src/lib.rs:1119-1152`):
```rust
/// A symbol kind.
#[derive(Eq, PartialEq, Copy, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(transparent)]
pub struct SymbolKind(i32);
lsp_enum! {
impl SymbolKind {
    pub const FILE: SymbolKind = SymbolKind(1);
    pub const MODULE: SymbolKind = SymbolKind(2);
    // ... through TYPE_PARAMETER = 26
}
}
```
An unrecognized `SymbolKind(99)` deserializes fine; the generated `Debug` impl has a `_ => write!(f, "{}({})", stringify!($typ), self.0)` arm (`src/lib.rs:70-79`). Only the *string* `TryFrom<&str>` conversion can error.

The spec rationale is carried verbatim in the doc comment (`src/lib.rs:1154-1166`):
```rust
/// Specific capabilities for the `SymbolKind` in the `workspace/symbol` request.
pub struct SymbolKindCapability {
    /// The symbol kind values the client supports. When this
    /// property exists the client also guarantees that it will
    /// handle values outside its set gracefully and falls back
    /// to a default value when unknown.
    pub value_set: Option<Vec<SymbolKind>>,
}
```

There's also a revealing comment at `src/lib.rs:740-753` — they *wanted* `#[serde(other)]` on an internally-tagged enum for the fallback case and couldn't have it (serde-rs/serde#912), so they fell back to `untagged`:
```rust
// TODO: Once https://github.com/serde-rs/serde/issues/912 is solved
// we can remove ResourceOp and switch to the following implementation
// of DocumentChangeOperation:
//
// #[serde(tag = "kind", rename_all="lowercase" )]
// pub enum DocumentChangeOperation {
//     Create(CreateFile), Rename(RenameFile), Delete(DeleteFile),
//     #[serde(other)]
//     Edit(TextDocumentEdit),
// }
```

README CONTRIBUTING section says only: *"If you are making a change which adds, removes or modifies the LSP API it is highly appreciated if you link to the spec where this change is described."*

